ocsr: opencv-python-headless. Apply from the repository root with
deploy/patches/apply-pyproject-patches.sh (git apply, then `uv lock`). If git apply refuses because
services/ocsr/pyproject.toml has moved on, make the same two edits by hand (the added dependency line
and the [tool.uv] table below) and run `uv lock` in services/ocsr.

opencv: opencv-python's cv2 needs libGL.so.1, libglib2.0-0, libgthread, libX11 and libxcb at import
time (checked with ldd on the locked wheel); the headless wheel of the same version needs only libc, libm,
libgcc_s and libstdc++ (checked the same way). With it the runtime image has no apt packages at all,
no X11 or mesa libraries to patch, and the wheel is 13 MB smaller. The service never opens a window.
Both wheels provide the module cv2, so installing both would clash: the direct dependency becomes the
headless wheel at the locked version (4.11.0.86) and [tool.uv] exclude-dependencies removes
opencv-python from the resolution altogether (it is not in uv.lock; an override with a marker that never
matches would leave an sdist-only entry that a later `uv lock` fails to build, see seg-pyproject.patch).
Without this patch, build the image with --build-arg OPENCV_GUI_LIBS=1 instead.
decimer 2.8.0 imports cv2 and declares opencv-python, so this project had no direct opencv dependency:
the patch adds the headless wheel as one. ocsr needs no build pins: every dependency has a wheel.

--- a/services/ocsr/pyproject.toml
+++ b/services/ocsr/pyproject.toml
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
     "decimer==2.8.0",
     "decimer-service",
     "numpy==1.26.3",
+    "opencv-python-headless==4.11.0.86",
     "pillow>=13.0.0",
     "protobuf==4.25.1",
     "pystow",
@@ -48,6 +49,16 @@
     "slow: runs the real DECIMER Image Transformer on the golden crops (CPU, TensorFlow); select with -m slow",
 ]
 
+[tool.uv]
+# decimer imports cv2 but never opens a window. opencv-python also needs libGL.so.1 and
+# libglib2.0-0 at import time; the headless wheel does not, so the runtime image needs no apt
+# packages. decimer declares opencv-python, so it is excluded from the resolution (not locked at
+# all) and the headless wheel, pinned to the version that was locked before, is a direct dependency
+# and provides cv2 instead. Not an override with a marker that never matches: that leaves an
+# sdist-only opencv-python entry in uv.lock, which every later `uv lock` has to build to read its
+# metadata (see the seg patch).
+exclude-dependencies = ["opencv-python"]
+
 [tool.uv.sources]
 decimer-service = { path = "../common", editable = true }
 
